# Fair Credit Community Licence 1.0

SPDX-style identifier: `FCCL-1.0` (not an SPDX-registered licence)

**Summary (for convenience only; if it differs from the numbered terms, the numbered terms win).** People can use, change and share this software for anything, free. Organisations can use it free too, but must say publicly that they use it. Nobody may sell the software itself, or a service that is mainly the software. A company that builds the software into its own product and sells that product may do so, if it credits the software and links to its repository. The software collects no data about you.

---

## 1. Acceptance

You get a licence under these terms only by agreeing to them. Using, copying, changing or sharing the software means You agree. These terms are both promises You make and conditions of every licence You receive.

## 2. Definitions

In these terms, "including" means "including without limitation".

- **Licensor**: SamuelNDCE, the author, and anyone the author names as a co-owner of the software in its repository.
- **Software**: the work these terms came with (Meerkat), in the version You received, including its documentation, and any part of it.
- **You**: whoever is using the software. If You use it for an Organisation, the Organisation is also "You" for sections 6, 7 and 8.
- **Individual**: a natural person using the software for themselves, including for their own unpaid projects, study, research and hobbies, and for freelance or self-employed work that person does alone, without employees.
- **Organisation**: a company, partnership, charity, school, public body or any other group of people, and anyone using the software for the work or benefit of one. An employee or contractor who uses the software at work does so for the Organisation they work for, so that Organisation is the one with the duties below.
- **Affiliate**: an organisation that controls, is controlled by, or is under common control with another. **Control** means owning more than half of an entity's shares or assets, or the power to direct its management and policies by vote, contract or otherwise, directly or indirectly. An Organisation and its Affiliates count as one Organisation under these terms.
- **Sell**: to receive money or anything else of value in exchange for the software, for a copy of it, or for access to it. A subscription, a licence fee, a "pro" or "enterprise" edition, a paid download, and a hosted or managed service are all selling.
- **Hosted service**: a service where someone other than the customer runs the software and gives the customer access to any substantial set of its features or functionality, or to the results of running it.
- **Product**: a program, service or device that has its own purpose and that includes, runs or depends on the software as one part among others.
- **Extension**: a separate work, such as a pack of extra checks, that uses the software only through its documented plug-in interface (the configuration file, packs and check format) and does not copy the software's own code. An Extension is not a Modified Version.
- **Modified Version**: the software with changes to its own files, or a work that copies a substantial part of the software's files.
- **Contribution**: anything You intentionally send to the Licensor for inclusion in the software, in any form, including through the repository's issues and pull requests, except what You clearly mark in writing as "Not a Contribution".
- **Credit Notice**: the statement in section 6.
- **Share**: to give a copy of the software to anyone outside Your own Organisation, in any medium, in source or compiled form, including by making it available to download.

## 3. Copyright licence

The Licensor grants You a worldwide, free, non-exclusive licence under the Licensor's copyright to use, run, study, copy, change, make derivative works of, and Share the software, for any purpose that these terms do not forbid, for as long as You follow these terms. Your right to Share and to change the software is subject to section 7, and Your right to charge for anything is subject to section 8.

Anyone who gets the software from You gets their licence **directly from the Licensor**, on these same terms. You cannot sublicense it, and You cannot give anyone better terms or worse ones.

## 4. Patent licence

The Licensor grants You a licence under any patent claims the Licensor can license, or becomes able to license, that You would otherwise infringe by using the software as provided, for the purposes that section 3 allows.

If You make a written claim, including in a lawsuit or counterclaim, that the software infringes a patent, Your patent licence under this section ends immediately. If Your Organisation makes such a claim, the patent licence ends immediately for work done for that Organisation.

## 5. Individuals

An Individual has no duties under these terms except those in section 7 when they Share a copy, and the rule against selling in section 8.

## 6. Organisations: say that You use it

An Organisation may use the software inside the Organisation, on any number of machines, free of charge. It must, **within 60 days of first using the software and for as long as it keeps using it**, publish a Credit Notice somewhere the public can read, such as a credits, "open source" or "acknowledgements" page of its website or product, its public documentation, or the README of a public repository. The notice must say, in words at least as clear as:

> This organisation uses Meerkat, by SamuelNDCE (https://github.com/SamuelNDCE/Meerkat), under the Fair Credit Community Licence.

1. It may add its own words, but must not state or suggest that the Licensor endorses it.
2. One notice may cover an Organisation and its Affiliates if it names them or says it covers them.
3. If an Organisation truly cannot publish anything (for example because of a legal duty of secrecy), it must ask the Licensor in writing for permission to place the notice in its customer documentation instead. Without that written permission, the duty stands.
4. The duty ends 6 months after the Organisation stops using the software and removes it from its systems.

## 7. Sharing copies

If You Share the software, whether changed or not, You must:

1. give each recipient a copy of these terms, or the web address https://github.com/SamuelNDCE/Meerkat where they can be read;
2. keep every `Required Notice:` line, copyright notice and licence notice that came with the software;
3. if You changed it, mark each changed file with a prominent notice that You changed it, saying who and when, and not suggest that the Licensor made or approves the changed version; and
4. if You Share a Modified Version, make its complete source available to each recipient, free of charge, under these terms.

Sharing a copy inside Your own Organisation is not Sharing for item 4.

## 8. Selling, and building it into a Product

1. **Not allowed, unless You have a commercial licence under section 12:** to Sell the software, a Modified Version, or any copy of either; or to provide a Hosted service.
2. **Allowed:** to include the software in a Product and Sell the Product, if all of these are true: (a) the Product is more than the software and has its own purpose; (b) the Product's documentation and its website listing say plainly that it includes or uses Meerkat by SamuelNDCE and link to https://github.com/SamuelNDCE/Meerkat; and (c) the copy of the software inside the Product keeps these terms and every notice that came with it.
3. **Allowed:** to charge for services around the software that are Yours: consulting, training, support, installation, writing Extensions or harnesses for a client, or running the software for a client as part of delivering Your own work, so long as You are not simply giving the client access to the software or its results as a service.
4. **Allowed:** to make and Sell Extensions under any licence You choose. An Extension is Your own work.
5. The reports, logs and screenshots the software produces about Your project belong to You.
6. The test is what the customer is mainly paying for. If it is the software's own job, that is selling the software. Using affiliates, contractors, resellers, a free tier that leads to a paid one, or any other arrangement to get around this section is a breach of it.

## 9. Built for people: no telemetry, open connections, AI by choice

1. **No telemetry.** The software does not collect or send data about You, Your projects or Your machine, and the Licensor will not add anything that does. A Modified Version that You Share must not add telemetry either, unless the person running it has clearly switched it on (an opt-in that is off by default), and it says in plain words what is sent and where.
2. **Open connections.** Anyone may connect the software to any application's test harness, including applications made by the Licensor or by Perpetual Technologies, and may write Extensions and harnesses for it. An Organisation that does so has the same duties as any other under sections 6 and 8, so the Licensor's own companies credit and link to the repository like everyone else.
3. **AI is optional and visible.** Checks that use an AI model are off unless You turn them on. They say, before they run, what they will send and to which model, and they do not run at all when no model can be reached or no key or login is found. The software never sends code to a model on its own.

## 10. Names and logos

These terms give no right to the name "Meerkat" as the name of Your own product or service, or to the pixel-art meerkat mascot or the project's logos, except to say truthfully what Your work is or works with ("compatible with Meerkat", "includes Meerkat"), and to publish the Credit Notice and the credit in section 8.

